# SYNOPSIS

```c
#include <croi/syscall.h>

/* CROI_SYS_PORT_CREATE */
int64_t port_create(uint64_t options, uint32_t *out);
/* CROI_SYS_PORT_QUEUE */
int64_t port_queue(uint32_t port, const croi_port_packet_t *packets,
                   uint64_t count);
/* CROI_SYS_PORT_WAIT */
int64_t port_wait(uint32_t port, uint64_t deadline, croi_port_packet_t *packets,
                  uint64_t count, uint64_t leeway);
/* CROI_SYS_PORT_CANCEL */
int64_t port_cancel(uint32_t port, uint32_t source, uint64_t key);
/* CROI_SYS_OBJECT_BIND */
int64_t object_bind(uint32_t port, croi_bind_item_t *items, uint64_t count);

int64_t croi_port_queue(uint32_t port, const croi_port_packet_t *packet);
int64_t croi_port_wait(uint32_t port, uint64_t deadline,
                       croi_port_packet_t *packet, uint64_t leeway);
```

# DESCRIPTION

A port is the one thing a thread waits on.
Event sources (signals on any object, counters, interrupts, deadline overruns, memory pressure) are bound to a port with `object_bind`, and each event arrives as a packet that `port_wait` takes.
A wait on a port nothing is bound to, with a deadline, is a sleep.

A packet is 48 bytes:

```c
typedef struct {
  uint64_t key;
  uint32_t type;
  int32_t status;
  uint64_t payload[4];
} croi_port_packet_t;
```

The <key> is the one given when the packet's source was bound, or queued.
The <type> says what <payload> holds:

| Type | Value | From | Payload |
| `CROI_PKT_TYPE_USER` | 0 | port_queue | the caller's |
| `CROI_PKT_TYPE_SIGNAL` | 1 | a SIGNALS binding | [0] the trigger in the low 32 bits and the observed signals in the high, [1] count (persistent), [2] time (with TIMESTAMP) |
| `CROI_PKT_TYPE_INTERRUPT` | 7 | an interrupt | [0] the time in ns |
| `CROI_PKT_TYPE_PAGE_REQUEST` | 9 | a pager VMO | [0] the command in the low 16 bits and flags above, [1] offset, [2] length |
| `CROI_PKT_TYPE_BUDGET_OVERRUN` | 0x80 | an overrun binding | [1] the context's total overruns, [2] since the packet was last read |
| `CROI_PKT_TYPE_MEMORY_PRESSURE` | 0x81 | a pressure binding | [0] limit, [1] bytes charged, [2] changes since last read, [3] level |
| `CROI_PKT_TYPE_COUNTER` | 0x82 | a VALUE binding | [0] the value waited for, [1] the value reached, [2] time in ns |
| `CROI_PKT_TYPE_COUNTER_CHANGE` | 0x83 | a CHANGE binding | [1] the value, [2] count, [3] time in ns |

## port_create

`port_create` writes a handle to a new port to <out>.
It has READ, WRITE, TRANSFER and DUPLICATE, but not WAIT: nothing waits on a port.
<options> is any of:

: `CROI_PORT_BIND_TO_INTERRUPT`
  The port may be bound to interrupts (INTERRUPT bindings).
: `CROI_PORT_RING`
  The port delivers into a ring.
  <out> then holds two handles, `out[0]` the port and `out[1]` a VMO of one page (READ, WRITE, MAP) that holds the ring.

Another option is `CROI_ERR_INVALID_ARGS`.
The job's policy for new ports applies.

A ring port's ring is the ring of ring.h: `CROI_PORT_RING_SLOTS` (64) slots of `croi_port_packet_t`, format `CROI_PORT_RING_FORMAT`.
The reader maps the VMO read-write, attaches as the ring's consumer and takes packets from it with no call.
Packets the ring has no room for wait in the port and go in as the reader frees slots, at the next packet or the next `port_wait`.
A packet in the ring is the reader's: `port_cancel` no longer reaches it.

## port_queue

`port_queue` queues up to <count> (1 to 64) user packets, read from <packets>, on a port with WRITE.
The type of each is made `CROI_PKT_TYPE_USER`.
It stops at the first packet that cannot be queued and returns how many it queued; if the first fails it fails with that status.
A port holds at most 4096 user packets; one more fails with `CROI_ERR_SHOULD_WAIT`, and raises a policy exception (job(2)) as well.

`croi_port_queue` queues one and returns 0 or the status.

## port_wait

`port_wait` waits on a port with READ until it holds a packet, or until <deadline>, and writes up to <count> (1 to 64) packets to <packets>.
It takes the first packet, waiting for it if there is none, and then as many more as are already there, with no further wait.
It returns how many it took.
At the deadline with the port still empty it fails with `CROI_ERR_TIMED_OUT`.
The wait may end up to <leeway> nanoseconds after the deadline, never before it, so that timers can coalesce; a thread with a deadline profile always gets none.
A wait that a suspension (task(2)) interrupts is made again when the thread resumes.

On a ring port <count> must be 0 and <packets> is not used: the call delivers what is queued, then waits until the ring is not empty and returns 0.
It fails with `CROI_ERR_BAD_STATE` once the reader has broken the ring (its cursor passed the kernel's).
A ring port refuses a count other than 0, and any other port refuses 0, with `CROI_ERR_INVALID_ARGS`.

`croi_port_wait` takes one packet and returns 0 or the status.

## port_cancel

`port_cancel` needs WRITE on the port.
With <source> a handle (it needs WAIT), it removes the bindings made through that handle to this port under <key>, and the packets of them still queued.
With <source> 0 it removes, whatever the source, every binding to the port with <key> and every queued user packet with it; the packets of interrupts and other kernel sources are not removed.
If nothing was removed the call fails with `CROI_ERR_NOT_FOUND`.
Closing a handle (handle(2)) removes the SIGNALS, VALUE and CHANGE bindings made through it.

## object_bind

`object_bind` binds up to 64 event sources to a port with WRITE, in order.
Each item is:

```c
typedef struct {
  uint32_t handle;    // the event source
  uint32_t options;   // a kind | flags
  uint64_t key;       // the packets' key
  uint64_t trigger;   // SIGNALS: the signals; VALUE: the value; else 0
  int32_t status;     // out: 0, or why this item failed
  uint32_t reserved;  // 0
} croi_bind_item_t;
```

A nonzero <reserved> is `CROI_ERR_INVALID_ARGS`.
The call stops at the first item that fails, writes the status of each item it processed and returns how many were bound.
A bad port, a bad pointer or a count of 0 or over 64 fails the call before anything is bound.
The low byte of <options> is the kind, and the flags are or-ed in:

: `CROI_BIND_SIGNALS`
  Any object with the WAIT right.
  <trigger> is a mask of signals.
  Once (no PERSISTENT): one packet when any of the signals is asserted, at once if one is.
  With `CROI_BIND_PERSISTENT`: a packet each time one of them rises, and at binding if one is already set.
  A persistent packet is coalesced while it waits to be read, and payload[1] counts the rises; the reader takes packets until the object would block.
: `CROI_BIND_VALUE`
  A counter (counter(2)) with WAIT and READ, once.
  <trigger> is a signed value; one packet when the counter is at least that, at once if it is.
  `CROI_BIND_PERSISTENT` is refused.
: `CROI_BIND_CHANGE`
  A counter with WAIT and READ, persistent: a packet at every add.
  It is the doorbell of a ring.
: `CROI_BIND_INTERRUPT`
  An interrupt (interrupt(2)) with READ, persistent, to a port made with `CROI_PORT_BIND_TO_INTERRUPT` (`CROI_ERR_WRONG_TYPE` otherwise).
  An interrupt binds to one port at a time (`CROI_ERR_ALREADY_BOUND`).
: `CROI_BIND_OVERRUN`
  A thread with MANAGE_THREAD and a deadline profile, or an interrupt with WRITE and a handed-off context (profile(2)), persistent: a packet each period the budget runs out, coalesced.
  A thread or interrupt without such a context is `CROI_ERR_BAD_STATE`.
  Binding again replaces the port.
: `CROI_BIND_PRESSURE`
  A memory account (account(2)) with WRITE, persistent: a packet at every change of pressure level, coalesced, and one at once unless the level is normal.

The kinds other than SIGNALS and VALUE need `CROI_BIND_PERSISTENT` and a <trigger> of 0.
The flags are `CROI_BIND_PERSISTENT`, `CROI_BIND_EDGE` (SIGNALS: no packet at once for a signal already set) and `CROI_BIND_TIMESTAMP` (SIGNALS: payload[2] is the time of the event).
Any other flag is `CROI_ERR_INVALID_ARGS`.

The signals an object asserts are in `<croi/syscall.h>`: for a channel `CROI_SIGNAL_READABLE`, `CROI_SIGNAL_WRITABLE` and `CROI_SIGNAL_PEER_CLOSED`; for a process, thread or job `CROI_SIGNAL_TASK_TERMINATED` and the running, no-jobs and no-processes signals; for a VMO `CROI_VMO_ZERO_CHILDREN`; for a counter `CROI_COUNTER_POSITIVE` and `CROI_COUNTER_NON_POSITIVE`.

A port counts its bindings: past 50000 a binding still succeeds and raises a policy exception.

